WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.094
Deep networks memorize training data,

00:00:03.174 --> 00:00:06.852
and memorization ties to generalization and privacy.

00:00:06.932 --> 00:00:10.610
Feldman's memorization score is rigorous but costly,

00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:15.308
while input loss curvature is a cheap proxy nobody
could justify.

00:00:15.388 --> 00:00:20.728
This Purdue ICML 2024 paper builds the missing
theory linking memorization,

00:00:20.808 --> 00:00:24.920
curvature, and differential privacy on CIFAR and ImageNet.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:29.912
Deep networks memorize training data, which matters
for generalization,

00:00:29.992 --> 00:00:32.291
noisy learning, and privacy leakage.

00:00:32.371 --> 00:00:37.643
Feldman's memorization score quantifies this
rigorously but is far too expensive.

00:00:37.723 --> 00:00:41.476
Input loss curvature, the trace of the input-loss Hessian,

00:00:41.556 --> 00:00:44.978
tracks memorization roughly a thousand times cheaper,

00:00:45.058 --> 00:00:47.622
yet the connection was purely empirical,

00:00:47.702 --> 00:00:51.190
with no theory linking either to differential privacy.

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:57.648
Proving curvature bounds memorization would license
the cheap proxy over the expensive score.

00:00:57.728 --> 00:01:00.231
Because memorization is a privacy risk,

00:01:00.311 --> 00:01:05.462
linking differential privacy to curvature explains
why privacy reduces leakage.

00:01:05.542 --> 00:01:11.025
Unlike influence functions, which assume Hessian
convexity that fails for deep nets,

00:01:11.105 --> 00:01:13.740
this framework needs no such assumptions.

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:18.886
The paper proves three links in a triangle
connecting memorization,

00:01:18.966 --> 00:01:22.286
input loss curvature, and differential privacy.

00:01:22.366 --> 00:01:25.396
First, curvature upper-bounds memorization.

00:01:25.476 --> 00:01:29.808
Second, the privacy parameter upper-bounds average
curvature.

00:01:29.888 --> 00:01:32.991
Third, privacy bounds memorization directly.

00:01:33.071 --> 00:01:37.837
None assume Hessian convexity, so they hold for
real deep networks,

00:01:37.917 --> 00:01:40.730
and all three are validated empirically.

00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:46.280
The theory starts from a second-order
Nesterov-Polyak expansion of the loss,

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:48.352
introducing the input Hessian.

00:01:48.432 --> 00:01:51.874
A zero-mean perturbation cancels first-order terms,

00:01:51.954 --> 00:01:58.575
and taking expectations bounds memorization by
expected curvature plus a data-independent offset.

00:01:58.655 --> 00:02:01.614
With the definition of differential privacy,

00:02:01.694 --> 00:02:04.446
a smaller epsilon forces lower curvature.

00:02:04.526 --> 00:02:09.350
Curvature itself is estimated cheaply via
Hutchinson's trace estimator.

00:02:09.730 --> 00:02:13.493
Validation uses CIFAR10, CIFAR100, and ImageNet.

00:02:13.573 --> 00:02:19.579
Memorization relies on Feldman and Zhang's
precomputed scores and ensembles,

00:02:19.659 --> 00:02:24.063
a thousand models on CIFAR100 and a hundred on ImageNet,

00:02:24.143 --> 00:02:26.865
using Small Inception and ResNet50.

00:02:26.945 --> 00:02:32.470
Privacy experiments train ResNet18 with DP-SGD
across several budgets.

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:34.344
Every prediction holds.

00:02:34.424 --> 00:02:41.390
Memorization plotted against input loss curvature
shows a strong linear trend on CIFAR100 and ImageNet,

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:43.375
especially clean on ImageNet.

00:02:43.455 --> 00:02:47.822
Stronger privacy drives curvature down along the
predicted curve,

00:02:47.902 --> 00:02:50.285
and for the most-memorized examples,

00:02:50.365 --> 00:02:56.100
memorization rises with the privacy budget while
staying under the theoretical bound.

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:02.108
Curvature uses Hutchinson's trace estimator with
step 1e-3 and ten Rademacher vectors,

00:03:02.188 --> 00:03:07.816
cheap enough to run at scale.

00:03:07.896 --> 00:03:13.524
Privacy sweeps epsilon over one, ten, twenty,
thirty, forty, and fifty at delta 1e-5,

00:03:13.604 --> 00:03:19.232
averaging ten seeds.

00:03:19.312 --> 00:03:24.940
The five hundred most-memorized CIFAR100 examples
collapse under strong privacy and recover as epsilon grows.

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:27.726
A few numbers capture the work.

00:03:27.806 --> 00:03:34.704
Input loss curvature is about three orders of
magnitude cheaper than the Feldman score.

00:03:34.784 --> 00:03:40.960
Ground truth averages a thousand models on CIFAR100
and a hundred on ImageNet.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:45.372
Privacy sweeps epsilon from one to fifty at delta 1e-5.

00:03:45.452 --> 00:03:48.660
Three theorems tie the triangle together.

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:53.518
The empirical curvature-memorization link now rests
on firm theory.

00:03:53.598 --> 00:04:00.662
Curvature provably upper-bounds memorization and is
itself bounded by the differential privacy parameter,

00:04:00.742 --> 00:04:02.091
closing the triangle.

00:04:02.171 --> 00:04:07.193
Needing no Hessian-convexity assumptions, it
applies to real deep networks,

00:04:07.273 --> 00:04:12.500
so cheap curvature is a justified proxy, and
privacy provably suppresses both.
